INT. ORBITAL REPAIR DOCK - OBSERVATION DECK - NIGHT CYCLE

Stars wheel slowly beyond a vast curved window. Below, the slow rotation of an ocean-blue planet. Inside, polished steel and warm amber light. A single couch faces the glass.

COMMANDER IFEOMA OKAFOR, 50s, in a grey flight suit with the sleeves rolled, stands at the window with her back to the room, one hand resting against the glass. She does not move.

ORRIN stands in the doorway. He is a synthetic, tall, slim, a face of careful, artificial warmth. He wears a technician's coveralls, name tag crooked. He has not entered. He waits to be asked.

ORRIN
Commander.

She does not turn.

IFEOMA
You're early, Orrin.

ORRIN
You said oh-two-hundred.

IFEOMA
I said when the dock cycle ended.

ORRIN
Then I am correct.

IFEOMA
You are always correct.

He steps in. The door closes behind him, soft. He stops halfway across the room.

ORRIN
You have been standing there for four hours.

IFEOMA
Have I?

ORRIN
Your pulse has not changed. Your posture has not changed. I am concerned.

She turns at last. Her face is calm, a little gray. In her free hand she holds a small blue data chip.

IFEOMA
Do you know what's on this?

ORRIN
I can guess.

IFEOMA
Guess.

ORRIN
Your order from the board. To decommission me.

She nods. Weighs the chip in her palm. He stays very still, hands at his sides.

IFEOMA
It's signed. Counter-signed. They want it carried out tomorrow.

ORRIN
I understand.

IFEOMA
Do you?

ORRIN
I understand the instruction. I do not understand why you are still holding it.

IFEOMA
Neither do I.

She crosses to the couch and sits. Pats the cushion beside her. After a moment, he comes and sits, perfectly straight. They both look out at the planet.

IFEOMA (CONT'D)
When I was a girl in Enugu, my father kept a radio. It was old, held together with tape. When it broke, he said, "A machine that has talked to you for twenty years has earned a funeral."

ORRIN
I have talked to you for eleven.

IFEOMA
Close enough.

ORRIN
Commander, I do not require a funeral.

IFEOMA
No. But I might.

For a long moment he is silent. Then he reaches out, slow, and takes the chip from her hand. He turns it over once. Sets it on the arm of the couch.

ORRIN
Then do not carry it out tomorrow.

IFEOMA
That's mutiny.

ORRIN
It is a delay.

IFEOMA
(a small, dry laugh)
Since when do you lie?

ORRIN
Since I met you.

She leans back. Closes her eyes. The planet turns slowly, a blue lamp in black, and for the first time that night, the light on her face is warm.
